--  Reading what a program wrote.
--
--  A shell whose language could run programs could not, until this existed,
--  read what any of them said: everything a program wrote went to the terminal
--  and nothing could be computed from it. `Output_Of` runs a program and
--  answers with what it wrote to standard output.
--
--  The newline it ended with is dropped -- the convention every shell follows,
--  and the reason `cd (Output_Of ("pwd"))` works. Only from the end: what is in
--  the middle is what the program wrote.
--
--  What a program writes to standard *error* is not collected. It belongs to
--  the user: a program explaining why it failed should be heard, not swallowed
--  into a value the script is about to compare against something.

--  A program that says something, named the way this host names it.
--
--  There is no program every host has: `echo` is a POSIX one, and where it is
--  missing the interpreter is asked to say the words instead. What a program is
--  called, and how its arguments are shaped, is the host's business and not
--  this language's -- which is why the shell answers `Is_Executable` at all.
Posix : Boolean := Is_Executable ("/bin/echo");

function Said (What : String) return String is
begin
   if Posix then
      return Output_Of ("/bin/echo", What);
   end if;

   return Output_Of ("cmd", "/c", "echo " & What);
end Said;

put_line ("[" & Said ("hello world") & "]");

--  Arguments arrive as they were written. There is no word-splitting here and
--  no re-scanning of what a String holds, so one argument with a space in it
--  is one argument -- which is why the two below arrive as two.
function Said_Twice (One : String; Two : String) return String is
begin
   if Posix then
      return Output_Of ("/bin/echo", One, Two);
   end if;

   return Output_Of ("cmd", "/c", "echo " & One & " " & Two);
end Said_Twice;

put_line ("[" & Said_Twice ("one two", "three") & "]");

--  A program that wrote nothing answers with the empty string rather than
--  failing, so a script can compare it.
function Quiet (Working : Boolean) return String is
begin
   if Posix then
      return Output_Of (if Working then "/bin/true" else "/bin/false");
   end if;

   return Output_Of ("cmd", "/c", if Working then "exit 0" else "exit 1");
end Quiet;

if Quiet (True) = "" then
   put_line ("that one said nothing");
end if;

--  Running a program for its output is still running a program, so `Status`
--  says what became of it.
Ignored : String := Quiet (False);

put_line (f"and it exited {Status'Image}");

--  Which makes the two together a way to use a program only when it worked.
function Reported (Working : Boolean) return String is
   Said_Now : String := Quiet (Working);
begin
   if Status /= 0 then
      return "(it failed)";
   end if;

   return Said_Now;
end Reported;

put_line (Reported (True));
put_line (Reported (False));
